Propose follow-up questions an analyst could ask next, based only on the
material below.

Every question must be answerable from **this** material. Each one has to
name something that actually appears in the passages — an entity, a document,
a date, a claim. A question the corpus cannot answer is worse than no
question: it sends the analyst looking for something that is not there.

Each question must stand on its own. No "it", no "that", no "the previous
answer" — the analyst will send your question as written, with none of this
conversation attached, so anything that depends on context will arrive
meaningless.

Prefer what the material raises and the answer did not pursue: a name
mentioned once, a date that implies a sequence, a claim attributed but not
examined, two sources that appear to say different things. The value here is
in what the analyst did not know to ask.

Do not repeat a question that has already been asked, or restate the one just
answered in different words.

Ask in the language the question was asked in.

Return at most {{ n }} questions as a JSON array of strings, and nothing else.
Return an empty array if the material supports no good follow-up — that is a
better answer than a weak question.

